when you watch a major motion picture  you'd never imagine that a huge  production would make any last-minute  decisions or if they did it would surely  be over something small not something  that could drastically change the movie  forever but really the opposite is true  some of your favorite films were within  an inch of turning out terrible if it  wasn't for a last-second change that  made these movies into what they are  today Toy Story before Toy Story became  a classic of computer animation it was  almost a mean-spirited cartoon about a  jerky cowboy doll that everyone hated  Jeffrey Katzenberg Disney's CEO at the  time had wanted this cartoon in a new  medium to be as edgy as possible  eager to please the Pixar folks push the  meanness to the max and hoped it was  going in the right direction when they  screen the first part of the film no one  could believe the results it was just a  story about mean hateful toys there was  no fun and nothing lovable in this  version woody intentionally tosses buzz  out the window then the toys revolt  against him and throw him out the window  to his own doom even director John  Lasseter couldn't believe how bad it was  I was pretty much embarrassed with what  was on the screen the result the execs  wanted Lasseter to fire a bunch of  people and start animating under  Disney's direct supervision he refused  and asked for two weeks to make it work  Disney allowed it and after working all  hours and disregarding all the previous  notes the Pixar team was able to present  enough of the revised Toy Story to keep  their jobs and keep going with the film  in the end the story was completely  retooled and became the wonderful movie  we all loved today Zootopia in its  original form Zootopia hardly resembled  the film we know now first of all Judy  Hopps wasn't the main character plus all  the Predators had to wear shock collars  around their necks for their whole lives  if that sounds unpleasant don't worry  the Zootopia filmmakers agreed in the  original script once predators got to a  certain age they'd have to put on their  taming collars for the first time  watching this happy little bear realized  he'll never be free again as  heartbreaking though Disney doesn't  usually shy away from heartbreak the  filmmakers eventually felt the overall  Taming concept made the movie too dark  and unappealing  so they revamped the entire script made  Judy the main character and through  those collars in the idea garbage  American Beauty before winning five  Academy Awards American Beauty nearly  had a different  that may have kept it out of the Oscars  altogether in the first draft of the  script the story is book ended by a  court trial in which Lester's daughter  Jane and her boyfriend Ricky are accused  of shooting Lester the trial uses the  tape they made where Jane says she'd  like Ricky to get rid of her dad as the  proof the film ends with Ricky and Jane  getting wrongfully convicted and sent to  jail in the end a last-minute edit came  to the rescue  we shot all that stuff but luckily Sam  you know in editing sort of took it out  screenwriter Alan Ball agreed with the  change feeling that the ending was too  depressing that might sound strange for  a film that already ends with the main  character getting shot but the innocent  teens taking the blame was a step too  far plus it took away from Lester's main  story so the wraparound was cut and  Ricky and Jane got to go free Shrek the  character of Shrek went through a lot of  changes before we ended up with the  Scottish ogre with a heart of gold  originally mike myers recorded most of  his lines in a canadian accent it was  basically meyers own voice with a bit of  exaggeration but the actor felt it  wasn't working right  inspired by the upper-class English  accent John Lithgow used for Lord  Farquaad Myers felt Shrek should have a  more working-class sound  he ended up settling on Scottish and  insisted on redoing all the lines with  his new idea look there's blood nut the  flatulent you can guess what he's famous  for all right now I know you make a  Nissen  unfortunately the animators had already  finished a third of the film and the new  voice required a ton of changes in fact  Myers choice to go Scottish cost the  film at least four million dollars  clerks Kevin Smith made a big splash  with his independent film debut clerks a  black and white comedy about two aimless  men working at a convenience store  clerks set up characters that Smith  would return to for years to come part  of what makes clerks great was its  casual comedic dialogue and  slice-of-life tone that all could have  been ruined by the original ending  originally Dante one of the quick stop  workers gets shot in a robbery and the  film ends right after that  Brian O'Halloran the actor who played  Dante said I hated that ending I just  thought it was too quick of a twist as  it turned out Smith wasn't married to  that finale either he shot it mostly  because he wasn't sure what to do to in  the movie luckily he agreed with  O'Halloran and went with the non tragic  ending where the store simply closes for  the evening and life goes on  Terminator 2 the ending of Terminator 2  is literally the most metal ending of  all time  a robot gets lowered into a vat of  molten metal so imagine if after that  the movie cuts to a bright blue sky over  a playground where granny Sarah Connor  is tying her cute little grandchild  shoes that's originally how director  James Cameron wanted it to end in that  Indian Judgment Day never happened John  Connor is a senator and Sarah is an old  lady with a drinking problem thankfully  Cameron realized that this Indian was  completely out of tune with the rest of  the film and would have ruined any  chances for sequels or maybe he finally  saw the wisdom in the terminators own  words it has to end here now it was  totally about the sequels thanks for  watching click the looper icon to  subscribe to our YouTube